# RME route keys and failure-log redaction (rule 222)

**Status:** GO scope of `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-1` (R-01, R-02), `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-2` (R-03..R-08),
`DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-3` (R-09..R-15) and `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-4` (R-16..R-23), all 2026-11-05. Sprint records:
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-1.md`, `docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-2.md`,
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-3.md`, `docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-4.md`. Owner approval: each
revision below, recorded in its sprint doc (CG-22). Policy: rule 221 §7 (RME revision programme). Category under the
Foundation-First Sprint Lock: hotfix / security / monitoring, all allowed before Foundation GO; the lock itself is
unchanged. Programs 3 and 4 EXTEND this rule (same contract, more parameters); neither supersedes anything.

## 1. R-01, R-03..R-15 — a route identifier that is not an identifier never reaches the database

1. These parameters are constrained globally by `App\Support\Routing\DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`. Each is
   domain-specific and every route carrying it binds a bigint `id()` primary key, implicitly (no model overrides the
   route key; there is no explicit `Route::bind`) or through an `int` argument looked up by key:

   | Parameter | Routes | Key | Binding | Revision |
   |---|---|---|---|---|
   | `clinicVisit` | 44 | `trx_clinic_visits.id` | implicit | R-01 (Program 1) |
   | `rmeInvoice` | 15 | `trx_rme_invoices.id` | implicit | R-03 |
   | `medicalRecord` | 7 | `trx_medical_records.id` | implicit | R-04 |
   | `rmePrescription` | 4 | `trx_rme_prescriptions.id` | implicit | R-05 |
   | `consent` | 3 | `trx_rme_visit_consents.id` | implicit | R-06 |
   | `odontogram` | 3 | `trx_odontograms.id` | implicit | R-07 |
   | `diagnosis` | 7 | `trx_medical_record_diagnoses.id` (2), `mst_clinical_diagnoses.id` (5) | implicit | R-09 (Program 3) |
   | `receiptSnapshot` | 1 | `trx_rme_receipt_snapshots.id` | `int` argument | R-10 |
   | `handwriting` | 2 | `trx_medical_record_handwritings.id` | implicit | R-12 |
   | `handwritingPage` | 2 | `trx_medical_record_handwriting_pages.id` (the page ROW's key, not an index) | implicit | R-13 |

   The pattern is `[1-9][0-9]{0,17}`: 1–18 digits, no sign, no leading zero. Every key an identity column issues is in that
   range, and 18 nines is below the bigint ceiling, so PostgreSQL can always cast what the router lets through.
2. Why: implicit model binding runs `where "id" = '<segment>'`. On PostgreSQL a non-integer segment raises
   SQLSTATE 22P02 and a number beyond bigint raises 22003, so the request answered HTTP 500 and wrote an ERROR line
   instead of a 404. SQLite compares loosely and finds no row, so the defect is invisible on SQLite. It was live in
   production, where every such line holds the monitoring log signal at WATCH.
3. `DatabaseRouteKey::register()` runs first in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`. A global pattern applies only to
   routes defined after it is registered; the compiled collection that `route:cache` writes carries it.
4. **Never add a per-route constraint for a parameter `DatabaseRouteKey` governs.** A route-level `where` /
   `whereNumber` OVERRIDES the global pattern. Four visit-bound legacy-archive routes carried
   `->whereNumber('clinicVisit')` (`[0-9]+`), which still admits a number beyond bigint; they were removed. Program 3
   removed three more (`receiptSnapshot` on the receipt reprint route, `handwriting` and `handwritingPage` on the
   oversight image routes). One authority per parameter.
5. Extending the global constraint to another DOMAIN-SPECIFIC parameter means adding its name to
   `DatabaseRouteKey::CONSTRAINED_PARAMETERS`; nothing else. Before adding one, prove from the code that every route
   carrying the name binds a bigint key, and that no route overrides it. A name may bind more than one model
   (`diagnosis` does) provided every one is a bigint `id()`.
6. **A GENERIC parameter name is never constrained globally** (`visit`, `patient`, `record`, `page`, `branch`, …): it
   may later name another identifier type, and a global numeric pattern would silently 404 that route. The oversight
   lookup already resolves a visit by its string visit number. Constrain a generic name per route with
   `->where('<name>', DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN)` (or `PAGE_NUMBER_PATTERN`, §1.11), reusing the constant (never a
   copied regex), and never with `whereNumber`, whose `[0-9]+` admits a number beyond bigint or PHP's integer range plus
   a leading-zero alias. Pin a generic name's contract on an explicit list of route NAMES, never on "every route
   carrying the name", so a future route may still use the name for another identifier type.
7. R-08: the five `oversight/rme/visits/{visit}` routes now carry `->where('visit', DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN)`.
   They are not model-bound: the controller takes `int $visit`. Under `whereNumber` a number beyond PHP's integer
   range failed the `int` conversion (TypeError, HTTP 500 plus ERROR), and `0<id>` cast to the real id, so a
   non-canonical URL opened — and audited — a real clinical record. 18 digits always fits a PHP integer.
8. **Implicit binding runs BEFORE the route's permission middleware.** So the unconstrained defect was reachable by
   any authenticated account, including one holding no permission, and a well-formed key that names no record is a
   404 from binding for everyone (the permission gate only ever sees a real record). The constraint keeps both
   orders intact: real record without permission → 403, no such record → 404, non-key segment → 404. Do not
   "fix" the binding order to make a permission 403 appear first. (An `int`-argument route has no binding, so its
   permission middleware answers first; a guest is redirected to login before the controller runs.)
9. A tightened constraint also tightens `PostAuthenticationRedirectService::matchGetRoute()`: an intended URL that
   no longer matches a route falls back to the default landing. That is the intended direction. Laravel's URL
   generator does not validate `wheres`, so `route(...)` calls built from real ids and stored page numbers are
   unaffected.
10. Program 3 per-route constraints (generic names, `int` arguments, measured 2026-11-05):
    - R-11 `{patient}` — the ONE oversight route `oversight.rme.patients.show` (`int $patient` → `mst_patients.id`):
      `DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`. The other `{patient}` routes (`settings.patients.*`, master data) were
      constrained later by Program 4 (R-17, §1.12).
    - R-14 `{record}` — all 19 legacy archive routes (`rme.legacy-records.*` ×6, `rme.legacy-odontograms.*` ×4,
      `oversight.legacy.*` ×9; `int $record` → `trx_rme_legacy_records.id` / `trx_odontogram_legacy_records.id`):
      `DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`.
    - R-15 `{page}` — all 6 page routes (the four record page routes and `settings.rme.legacy-imports.pages.show`,
      `settings.rme.legacy-odontograms.pages.show`): `DatabaseRouteKey::PAGE_NUMBER_PATTERN`.
    Each `whereNumber` admitted an overflow (TypeError on the `int` argument, HTTP 500 plus ERROR) and a leading-zero
    alias that opened — and AUDITED — a real patient history, archive or page.
11. **A page number is not a key.** `{page}` is a 1-based ordinal (the rasterizer numbers pages from 1) compared
    against `page_number`, declared `unsignedInteger` — a 4-byte PostgreSQL `integer` (ceiling 2,147,483,647).
    Reusing `PATTERN` (18 digits) would still let an 11-to-18-digit page through PHP's `int` into an int4 comparison,
    where PostgreSQL raises SQLSTATE 22003 (HTTP 500) — measured on PostgreSQL 16.15 under `whereNumber`.
    `PAGE_NUMBER_PATTERN` is `[1-9][0-9]{0,8}`: page 1 up to 999,999,999, always inside int4 and PHP's integer, `0`
    and a leading zero refused, a well-formed page beyond the document's last page a 404 from the lookup. Before
    reusing either pattern for a new parameter, check the COLUMN TYPE it is compared against, not only the PHP type.
12. Program 4 per-route constraints (R-16..R-23, generic names, measured 2026-11-05 on the live collection — 57
    parameter uses, every key a bigint `id()`, no model overriding its route key, so all take
    `DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`; pinned on route NAMES in `RemainingRouteKeyConstraintTest`):

    | Rev | Parameter | Routes | Key | Was |
    |---|---|---|---|---|
    | R-16 | `{import}` | 14 `settings.rme.legacy-imports.*` / `legacy-odontograms.*` | `int $import` → `stg_rme_legacy_imports.id`, `stg_odontogram_legacy_imports.id` | `whereNumber` |
    | R-17 | `{patient}` | 7 `settings.patients.*` (+ the R-11 oversight route) | `mst_patients.id`, implicit | none / `whereNumber` |
    | R-18 | `{document}` | 2 `settings.patients.documents.*` | `mst_patient_documents.id`, implicit | `whereNumber` |
    | R-19 | `{candidate}` | 5 `satusehat.submissions.*`, 2 `lab-case-candidates.*` | `trx_satusehat_candidates.id`, `trx_lab_case_candidates.id` (two models) | none |
    | R-20 | `{mapping}` | 5 `satusehat.mappings.*` | `mst_satusehat_code_mappings.id`, implicit | none |
    | R-21 | `{issue}` | 8 `satusehat.readiness.issues.*`, 3 `satusehat.branches.issues.*` | `int $issue` → `trx_satusehat_data_quality_issues.id` | none / `whereNumber` |
    | R-22 | `{identifier}` | 2 `satusehat.identifiers.*` | `mst_satusehat_entity_identifiers.id`, implicit (a database key, not an external IHS id) | none |
    | R-23 | `{doctor}` | 7 `settings.doctors.*` (incl. account links), `rme.doctor-branch-locks.release-session` | `mst_doctors.id`, implicit | none / `whereNumber` |

    Measured on PostgreSQL 16.15 before the change: 227 HTTP 500s (22P02, 22003, TypeError) across the matrix, and a
    leading-zero alias that OPENED every record type, DEACTIVATED a SATUSEHAT identifier, a patient and a doctor, and
    DELETED a KTP document. Use the resource form `Route::resource(...)->where(['<name>' => DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN])`
    for a resource; `PendingResourceRegistration::where()` applies it to every member route.
13. **Implicit binding also runs BEFORE `can:` (Laravel's `Authorize` middleware).** The SATUSEHAT group is gated by
    `can:satusehat.access` (Super Admin only), yet an unconstrained model-bound segment answered 500 to ANY signed-in
    account, because `SubstituteBindings` sorts ahead of `Authorize` in the middleware priority. A group-level gate is
    not a substitute for a route-key constraint. With the constraint the order of §1.8 holds for `can:` too.
14. **Constraining a key parameter also hardens a static sibling.** Several `whereNumber` constraints doubled as
    disambiguators (`create` before `{import}`, `requests` before `{doctor}`). `PATTERN` rejects letters too, so the
    disambiguation is kept; never replace such a constraint with nothing.
15. Still open (measured 2026-11-05, NOT approved, NOT done): every other route parameter is outside this rule's
    approved scope. Unconstrained or under `whereNumber` on 2026-11-05 (route uses): `labOrder` 22, `batch` 17,
    `branch` 17, `wave` 16, `labV2Order` 15, `product` 13, `doctorDevice` 11, `registration` 10, `correction` 8,
    `delivery` 8, `refundRequest` 7, `settlement` 6, and further inventory, lab, master-data and doctor-access names.
    Each needs its key type verified (a UUID or page number is not a bigint key) and its own owner approval.
    Inventory: `docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-4.md` §9.

## 2. R-02 — a caught exception is logged through the canonical redactor

1. Log a caught exception's text only through
   `App\Modules\Observability\Support\TelemetryRedactor::exceptionMessage()`, plus the exception class. Never
   `$e->getMessage()` raw into a log context.
2. Why: a `QueryException` message ends with ` (Connection: …, SQL: …)` with every binding interpolated, and
   PostgreSQL adds `DETAIL: Key (col)=(value)` / `Failing row contains (…)` lines carrying row values. A raw message
   can therefore write clinical values into the application log (CG-11). The redactor keeps the driver's error text
   (SQLSTATE included) and strips the rest.
3. Applied to the post-payment lab-candidate failure warning in `RmePaymentService`. That failure is still logged
   and still never rolls the payment back (Sprint 21 Phase 21.2, unchanged).
4. Reuse the redactor; never write another message sanitiser. A second copy drifts: the older
   `Str::before(…, ' (Connection:')` pattern strips the SQL tail but not the DETAIL row.
5. Limit, stated not hidden: the redactor removes quoted literals, NIK/phone digit runs, Nomor RM and dates; it
   cannot recognise an UNQUOTED free-text name in a non-database message. No current throw site under lab-candidate
   generation produces one. A new throw site that interpolates patient text must quote it or not include it.

